// Cs7.grammar — C# 7.0: tuples (T3.6.1).
//
// T0.3 merge semantics: re-declaring a rule name APPENDS alternatives (declaration order across
// files); it does NOT modify the Cs1 alternative. The engine is longest-match-wins and an
// equal-length tie resolves to the FIRST alternative (declaration order, Parser.cs:296-316). So a
// re-declared Cs7 alternative is reachable only when it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the existing ones.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser.cs):
//   * Tuple type — ParseTupleType (7920): "(" (ParseTupleElement,)+ ")" (list.Count < 2 ->
//     ERR_TupleTooFewElements). ParseTupleElement (7954): ParseType() + (IsTrueIdentifier() ?
//     ParseIdentifierToken() : null) — the element NAME comes AFTER the type (`int a` = type int,
//     name a). Entered from ParseUnderlyingType (7989) when the current token is "(".
//   * Tuple literal — ParseCastOrParenExpressionOrTuple (12828): "(" + first expression, then if
//     next is "," (12858) or first is Identifier + ":" (12866) -> ParseTupleExpressionTail (12882):
//     firstArg + ("," + argument)* (list.Count < 2 -> ERR_TupleTooFewElements). Each argument is an
//     expression, optionally with a name colon (Identifier ":" Expression, 12892-12897).
//   * Deconstruction — IsPossibleDeconstructionLeft (12231): "var" + "(" + ScanDesignator (12246) +
//     "="; ScanDesignator = identifier | "(" designator ("," designator)* ")". The deconstruction
//     ASSIGNMENT "(x, y) = t;" is an assignment EXPRESSION (ParseExpressionContinued, 11532) whose
//     LHS is a parenthesized variable designation — an EXPRESSION, not a separate statement.
//   * Item1 access — a regular member access ("." Identifier), handled by the Cs1 MemberAccess
//     postfix (Cs1.grammar:723). No change needed.
//
// CS7 only.

// === Tuple type (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 Type; the tuple-type alternative REQUIRES a comma
// (>= 2 elements), so it is mutually exclusive with a parenthesized type (a single type in parens,
// which is not a valid C# type — `(int)` is not a Type). Roslyn ParseTupleType (7920). ===
Type =
    | TupleType = "(" TupleTypeElement ("," TupleTypeElement)+ ")";

// A tuple type element: Type + optional name (Identifier). Roslyn ParseTupleElement (7954): the
// name comes AFTER the type (`int a` = type int, name a). The name is a true identifier
// (!ReservedKeyword). `int a` -> Type=int, name=a; `int` -> Type=int, no name.
TupleTypeElement = Type !ReservedKeyword Identifier?;

// === Tuple literal (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 Primary (append, T0.3 merge). The tuple literal
// REQUIRES a comma (>= 2 elements). Roslyn ParseCastOrParenExpressionOrTuple (12828) +
// ParseTupleExpressionTail (12882). ===
//
// Disambiguation with the Cs1 Parens ("(" Expression ")", Cs1.grammar:707):
//   * NAMED elements `(a: 1, b: 2)`: Parens FAILS (`a: 1` is not an Expression — ":" is not a
//     binary operator), so the TupleLiteral is the SOLE match. Clean version-purity (rejects at v6).
//   * UNNAMED elements `(1, 2)`: Parens MATCHES (the inner Expression absorbs the "," via the Comma
//     operator, bp 1, applicable at minPrecedence 0) as a parenthesized COMMA EXPRESSION, at the same
//     length as the TupleLiteral. The FIRST (Cs1 Parens) wins the tie -> parsed as a comma expression,
//     NOT a tuple literal. Consequence: the UNNAMED tuple literal PARSES at v6 (as a comma
//     expression), so its version-purity negative is BLOCKED (progress doc, Deviation D1). The
//     TupleLiteral alternative is dead for unnamed elements but used for named elements.
Primary =
    | TupleLiteral = "(" TupleLiteralElement ("," TupleLiteralElement)+ ")";

// A tuple literal element: an optional name (Identifier ":") + an Expression. Roslyn
// ParseTupleExpressionTail (12892-12897). The Expression is `Expression : Comma` (minPrecedence 1)
// so it does NOT absorb the "," separator (the TDOPP Comma fix, T3.3.2). Named union (the
// meta-grammar forbids `|` inside a group). Longest-match: `a: 1` -> NamedTupleElement (longer);
// `1` -> PositionalTupleElement (NamedTupleElement fails: 1 is not an Identifier).
TupleLiteralElement =
    | NamedTupleElement      = !ReservedKeyword Identifier ":" Expression : Comma
    | PositionalTupleElement = Expression : Comma;

// === Deconstruction (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 LocalVariableDeclaration (append, T0.3 merge). ===
// The deconstruction DECLARATION `var (x, y) = t;` REQUIRES the "(" after "var" (the
// DeconstructionVariableList), so it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the Cs2 "var" declaration
// (VariableDeclarator = identifier, which fails on "(") and the Cs1 Type-based declaration (var is
// reserved, not a Type). Roslyn IsPossibleDeconstructionLeft (12231) + ScanDesignator (12246).
//
// The deconstruction ASSIGNMENT `(x, y) = t;` is an assignment EXPRESSION (Roslyn
// ParseExpressionContinued, 11532) whose LHS is a parenthesized variable designation. In this
// grammar it is ALREADY parsed at every version via the Cs1 ExpressionStatement (as an Assign whose
// LHS is a parenthesized comma expression) — no new rule is required for it to parse (verified:
// parses at v6). It is NOT a separate statement rule here (progress doc, Deviation D2).
LocalVariableDeclaration =
    | DeconstructionDeclaration = "var" DeconstructionVariableList "=" Expression ";";

// The deconstruction variable list: "(" identifier ("," identifier)* ")". Roslyn ScanDesignator
// (12246) is recursive (nested deconstruction `((x, y), z)`); this grammar models the FLAT list
// required by the task (a documented simplification — nested deconstruction is out of scope, D3).
// Each element is a true identifier.
DeconstructionVariableList = "(" !ReservedKeyword Identifier ("," !ReservedKeyword Identifier)* ")";

// === Pattern matching (C# 7.0). ===
//
// The Cs1 `is` operator is a TDOPP POSTFIX (a binary operator at the Relational level), the named
// alternative `TypeIs = Expression : Relational "is" Type` (Cs1.grammar:662). The engine
// (Parser.BuildTdoppRulesInternal, Parser.cs:123-161) turns every alternative whose FIRST element is
// a self-Ref into a postfix operator; ParseRule (Parser.cs:223-335) applies ALL applicable postfixes
// greedily (ContinueFromPartialPostfix, Parser.cs:337-424) and keeps the LONGEST, with equal-length
// ties resolved to the FIRST postfix (declaration order). So a NEW `is` postfix that extends FURTHER
// than `TypeIs` wins for the longer pattern forms, while `TypeIs` still wins the equal-length tie for
// a bare type (`x is int`), keeping the C# 1.0 type pattern unchanged at every version.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser_Patterns.cs):
//   * ParseTypeOrPatternForIsOperator (21-31): the `is` RHS is a pattern; "Note that the syntax `_`
//     will be parsed as a type." (the discard in `is` becomes an IdentifierName/type).
//   * ParsePrimaryPattern (187-252) + ParsePatternContinued (284-398): discard (UnderscoreToken,
//     202-205), var (`var`+designation → VarPattern, 290-297), declaration (type+designation, 358-362),
//     type (367), constant (fallback expression, 250-251).
//   * Switch: CasePatternSwitchLabel(case, pattern, ParseWhenClause, colon) (LanguageParser.cs:10271-
//     10275); ParseWhenClause (10704-10714) = `when` + expression, BETWEEN the pattern and the `:`.

// === `is` pattern (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 Expression (append, T0.3 merge) to add a NEW postfix
// operator at the Relational level (the same level as TypeIs). For `x is int` the Cs1 TypeIs wins the
// equal-length tie (first declaration) → the C# 1.0 type pattern is unchanged. For `x is int y` /
// `x is 5` / `x is _` the TypeIsPattern is longer (or the sole match) → it wins. Roslyn
// ParseTypeOrPatternForIsOperator (21). ===
Expression =
    | TypeIsPattern = Expression : Relational "is" Pattern;

// A pattern (C# 7.0): discard, declaration (type + name), var, type, or constant (expression).
// Longest-match disambiguates: `int y` → DeclarationPattern (longer than TypePattern `int`); `5` →
// ConstantPattern (sole — a literal is not a Type); `var y` → VarPattern (sole — `var` is reserved,
// not a Type/Expression); `_` → DiscardPattern (first, wins the equal-length tie); `y` (a bare
// identifier) → TypePattern (first, wins the equal-length tie with ConstantPattern). Roslyn
// ParsePrimaryPattern (187) + ParsePatternContinued (284).
Pattern =
    | DiscardPattern     = "_"
    | DeclarationPattern = Type !ReservedKeyword Identifier
    | VarPattern         = "var" !ReservedKeyword Identifier
    | TypePattern        = Type
    | ConstantPattern    = Expression;

// === Pattern switch (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 CaseLabel (append, T0.3 merge). The Cs1
// CaseLabel = "case" Constant ":" (Cs1.grammar:901) handles `case 5:` / `case "a":` at every version.
// The new PatternCaseLabel handles `case int y:` / `case var y:` / `case 5:` and the guard
// `when Expression`. For `case 5:` the Cs1 CaseLabel wins the equal-length tie (first) → unchanged.
// For `case int y:` / `case var y:` / `case int y when ...:` the Cs1 CaseLabel fails (not a Constant)
// → PatternCaseLabel is the sole match. Roslyn CasePatternSwitchLabel (LanguageParser.cs:10271) +
// ParseWhenClause (10704). The guard is BETWEEN the pattern and the `:` (an Optional). ===
CaseLabel =
    | PatternCaseLabel = "case" Pattern ("when" Expression)? ":";
